Since there's no existing codebase, here's a practical email validation solution for a signup form:

**HTML & JavaScript:**

```html
<form id="signupForm">
  <input 
    type="email" 
    id="email" 
    name="email" 
    required
  />
  <button type="submit">Sign Up</button>
  <span id="emailError" class="error"></span>
</form>

<script>
  const form = document.getElementById('signupForm');
  const emailInput = document.getElementById('email');
  const errorSpan = document.getElementById('emailError');

  form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    errorSpan.textContent = '';

    const email = emailInput.value.trim();

    // Basic format check
    const emailRegex = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
    if (!emailRegex.test(email)) {
      errorSpan.textContent = 'Please enter a valid email address';
      return;
    }

    // Check length (RFC 5321)
    if (email.length > 254) {
      errorSpan.textContent = 'Email is too long';
      return;
    }

    // Passes validation, submit form
    console.log('Form submitted with email:', email);
    // form.submit(); // Uncomment to actually submit
  });
</script>
```

**Key features:**
- **Regex check**: validates basic email format (`something@domain.ext`)
- **Length validation**: enforces RFC 5321 limit of 254 chars
- **Trimming**: removes accidental whitespace
- **Error feedback**: displays clear message to user
- **Prevents submission**: until validation passes

For production, also validate on the server side—client-side validation is for UX, not security.
